@@ editorial
## Approach: capacity doubling

```c
struct IntVec {
    int *data;
    int size;       // elements in use
    int capacity;   // elements that fit
};

void vecPush(IntVec *v, int value) {
    if (v->size == v->capacity) {
        int newCap = v->capacity ? v->capacity * 2 : 4;
        int *bigger = realloc(v->data, newCap * sizeof *bigger);
        if (bigger == NULL) return;      // keep the old array on failure
        v->data = bigger;
        v->capacity = newCap;
    }
    v->data[v->size++] = value;
}

void vecFree(IntVec *v) {
    if (v == NULL) return;
    free(v->data);    // the elements
    free(v);          // the struct itself
}
```

### Why doubling is O(1) on average
With doubling, the array is copied when it holds 4, 8, 16, ... elements. Over `n` pushes the total number of copied elements is at most `4 + 8 + ... + n < 2n`, so each push costs O(1) *amortized*. Growing by a constant amount instead (`capacity + 10`) copies about n²/20 elements in total.

### `realloc` may move the block
`realloc` can resize in place, or allocate a new block, copy, and free the old one. LeakCode's allocator always moves, so a pointer kept into the old array (`int *first = &v->data[0];`) is immediately reported as a use-after-free.

### `realloc(NULL, n)`
Starting with `data = NULL` is fine: `realloc(NULL, n)` behaves like `malloc(n)`.

**Complexity:** amortized O(1) per push, O(1) get/set/pop; memory O(capacity) ≤ 2n.

