@@ editorial
## Approach: head index + count

```c
struct RingBuffer { int *items; int capacity; int head; int count; };

bool rbEnqueue(RingBuffer *q, int value) {
    if (q->count == q->capacity) return false;
    q->items[(q->head + q->count) % q->capacity] = value;   // slot after the last
    q->count++;
    return true;
}

bool rbDequeue(RingBuffer *q) {
    if (q->count == 0) return false;
    q->head = (q->head + 1) % q->capacity;                  // forget the front
    q->count--;
    return true;
}

int rbRear(const RingBuffer *q) {
    return q->count ? q->items[(q->head + q->count - 1) % q->capacity] : -1;
}
```

### Why a count?
With only `head` and `tail` indices, "full" and "empty" both look like `head == tail`. Keeping a `count` (or wasting one slot) removes the ambiguity.

### No moving, no allocating
Dequeuing from the front of a normal array means shifting every element (O(n)). The ring buffer just moves `head`. After `rbCreate`, the memory footprint never changes, which matters in embedded and real-time code where `malloc` at the wrong moment is not allowed.

**Complexity:** O(1) per operation, O(capacity) memory.
